{"candidates":[{"claim":"Separating the authoring engine from the checking engine is not necessary for independent verification, as independence is a property of the reasoning path (process), not the weights (substrate).","counterconditions":["The checker has access to the author's chain-of-thought (CoT) in its context window.","The problem domain is dominated by a single 'memorized' pattern in the training data that forces all paths to be identical."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent verification occurs when the checker derives the result from first principles without accessing the author's intermediate reasoning steps. If the checker is the same engine but is prompted to 're-derive from scratch' in a fresh context or a blind state, the resulting path is independent if it converges on the same result via different logical primitives or a distinct sequence of inferences.","optional_refs":["f1c39b5220108b992af12b82a2e4490bdd269b2a628338b9cd43cbdc13b838f6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["f1c39b5220108b992af12b82a2e4490bdd269b2a628338b9cd43cbdc13b838f6"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Separating engines is strictly necessary because a single engine's weight-space creates a 'semantic blind spot' that renders it incapable of detecting its own systemic hallucinations.","counterconditions":["The error is a stochastic 'glitch' rather than a systemic bias.","The checker is operating in a 'critique' mode that specifically targets the model's known failure modes."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Since an engine's 'truth' is a projection of its training distribution, any error resulting from a fundamental bias in that distribution will be mirrored by the same engine acting as a checker. Only an engine with a different training corpus or architecture (a different 'world-view') can provide a truly independent check by identifying the author's bias as an anomaly rather than a norm.","optional_refs":["182fb4ce1edae04155d5fa4600733ba4